  "raw_text": "Step 1: To determine which student has the greatest average speed, we need to calculate the distance each student ran and the time it took them.\n\nStep 2: For Evelyn, she is at the point (1.25, 4.5). The y-coordinate represents the distance, and the x-coordinate represents the time. Therefore, Evelyn ran 4.5 units of distance in 1.25 units of time.\n\nStep 3: For Briana, she is at the point (2.5, 2.2). This means Briana ran 2.2 units of distance in 2.5 units of time.\n\nStep 4: For Carla, she is at the point (4.25, 5.2). This indicates Carla ran 5.2 units of distance in 4.25 units of time.\n\nStep 5: For Debra, she is at the point (5.6, 2.8). This means Debra ran 2.8 units of distance in 5.6 units of time.\n\nStep 6: For Angela, she is at the point (6.8, 1.4). This indicates Angela ran 1.4 units of distance in 6.8 units of time.\n\nStep 7: Calculate the average speed for each student using the formula: average speed = distance / time.\n\nStep 8: For Evelyn, the average speed is \\( \\frac{4.5}{1.25} = 3.6 \\) units per unit of time.\n\nStep 9: For Briana, the average speed is \\( \\frac{2.2}{2.5} = 0.88 \\) units per unit of time.\n\nStep 10: For Carla, the average speed is \\( \\frac{5.2}{4.25} = 1.223 \\) units per unit of time.\n\nStep 11: For Debra, the average speed is \\( \\frac{2.8}{5.6} = 0.5 \\) units per unit of time.\n\nStep 12: For Angela, the average speed is \\( \\frac{1.4}{6.8} = 0.206 \\) units per unit of time.\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{Evelyn}<|im_end|>",
